Bare `hllc`, with no subcommand, prints help and exits non-zero (#129).
It used to lex whatever path followed it and dump the token stream,
which meant the first command anyone typed answered a question nobody
had asked. This transcript is what pins the whole command surface as
text: adding, renaming, or re-describing a subcommand shows up here as
a reviewable diff.

```console
$ hllc
? 2
Compile hl-lang (.hll) sources into Docker Compose YAML

Usage: hllc <COMMAND>

Commands:
  build   Compile into Compose YAML
  check   Run the same pipeline as `build`, writing nothing
  parse   Parse one file and pretty-print its AST
  tokens  Lex one file and print its token stream
  help    Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
  -h, --help     Print help (see more with '--help')
  -V, --version  Print version

EXAMPLES:
    hllc build jellyfin.hll                           Print Compose YAML to stdout
    hllc build jellyfin.hll --out docker-compose.yml  Write it to a file
    hllc build services/ --out dist/                  Build every service in a directory
    hllc check services/                              Compile it all, write nothing

See the book for the full field reference and directory-build modes:
https://travisboettcher.github.io/hl-lang/

```

`--out` and `--force` belong to `build` alone, and that is now a fact
about where they are declared rather than a rule `clap` has to enforce
(#123 added `requires`/`conflicts_with` for this; a subcommand cannot
also be a different subcommand, so those states stopped being
expressible at all):

```console
$ hllc check services --out dist
? 2
error: unexpected argument '--out' found

  tip: to pass '--out' as a value, use '-- --out'

Usage: hllc check <FILE>

For more information, try '--help'.

```
